title: Log context summaries
tags: Storage [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: What the log says of a log context without reading one of its operations — how many it holds, its first and last, who wrote it, which log contexts it reverts and which revert it — so that a client shows the log by log context and reads operations only for the log context it goes into.

A write to many records is one entry in the log, read back as an operation per record
([[Mass operations in the log]]): making up those operations costs a read of each
record's uuid. A client that shows the log as a list of log contexts — the log panel's top
level, `mf log contexts` — does not need them, so a summary of a log context is made of what
the keys of the log say alone.

!! What a summary holds

`GET /repos/:repo/log/contexts` ([[GET /repos/:repo/log/contexts]]) lists log contexts, newest
first — but not a context nothing is in, nor under it: a command that wrote nothing
(`mf reconcile` on an empty folder) or an answer left empty is no unit of work, and stays
in the store only until a reindex ([[Log contexts]]); whether one is empty is read off
its first operation, then its children's, stopping at the first found —, each with:

* its own `operations`, counted off `log_entries_by_context` — one key per entry, which holds its number of targets — and its `first_op` and `last_op`;
* when it was last written in (`updated`), its `origin` and whether it is the `watcher`'s, read off its first and last operations;
* how many `children` it has;
* `reverts` and `reverted_by`: the log contexts it undid and the log contexts that undid it, each with `whole` (the log context and its children undone, or some of its operations);
* `undone`: `null`, `partly` or `fully`, worked out from those marks alone.

The cost is a few keys per log context, whatever the number of operations in it. A log of ten
thousand operations written as one entry costs what a log of one operation does.

!! The marks

A revert says on its own log context which log contexts it reverts. The revert endpoint says it
whole when its target was a log context (undo, redo) and nothing was left out; whatever it
reverts is also said log context by log context, `whole: false`, from the operations it undid. The
store keeps both directions (`label_reverts`, [[Store tables]]), so a log context knows what
reverts it too, and both go with a log context that is pruned.

A log context is `fully` undone when a revert that undid it whole — or the log context it is
nested in — still stands, that is when that revert is not itself undone whole (a redo
stands the first change up again). It is `partly` undone when the reverts that stand
undid only some of its operations. Overlapping partial reverts can make `partly`
stand where `fully` would be exact, never the other way round.

!! The units, and going into one

`parent=none` (the default) lists the units of work, the log contexts nested in none;
`parent=<id>` the log contexts nested directly in one. The operations of a log context are read by
`GET /repos/:repo/log/contexts/:id`, which counts its operations off the keys as well and
makes up only those of the window asked for (`offset`, `limit`).

Log contexts are ordered by their id: the order they were opened in, which is the order they
were written in but for a log context that kept receiving writes (a long script's) after
others began.

`mf log contexts` ([[mf log contexts]]) prints them; `tests/kv_label_marks.rs` and
`tests/label_summaries_http.rs` hold the counts, the marks and the paging.
